# HMI website engineering rules

1. Read `HMI_WEBSITE_PRD.md` before changing features or architecture. The PRD is authoritative; record deviations in an ADR for human approval.
2. Work ONLY on the current explicitly approved milestone. M0/M1 before M2. Never self-approve a gate.
3. Begin by identifying acceptance criteria and writing failing automated tests. Follow RED → GREEN → REFACTOR → FULL SUITE → REVIEW.
4. Report only tests actually executed and outputs actually observed. If unavailable, state why, with blocker status; never invent green checks, links, visuals, or citations.
5. Never edit, push to, replace, or delete the historic repository `hmi-iiitd/hmi-iiitd.github.io` unless later explicit instruction authorizes a specific operation.
6. Never change `hmi.iiitd.edu.in`, DNS, GitHub custom domains, institutional server contents, or production deployment from Cursor without separate explicit release authorization.
7. Every new feature must have an acceptance test; every bug must gain a regression test before its fix.
8. Prefer Astro static rendering, typed structured data, progressive enhancement, and minimal JavaScript. Avoid major dependencies without ADR.
9. Build links and asset paths using the configured Astro `base`; test `/hmi-lab-website/` staging separately from `/` production.
10. No publication may ship without verified ordered authors, title, venue/status, year, evidence, and allowed resources. Don't fabricate missing DOIs, PDFs, code, datasets, awards, impact, or results.
11. Use real, approved lab research assets with alt/caption/license/consent metadata. Label illustrative figures; avoid generic decorative AI imagery on production.
12. Target WCAG 2.2 AA: semantic HTML, keyboard access, skip link, visible focus, proper contrast, captions, responsive reflow, `prefers-reduced-motion`.
13. No static-site PHP server dependencies. No public contact form in MVP; no secrets, participant data, or unpublished patient information in repo, Actions logs, or staging.
14. Do not silently broaden scope, rewrite research claims, or replace a working design with fashion-driven effects.
15. For each milestone PR include: test-first evidence, exact commands/results, responsive screenshots, a11y scan, content provenance, risks, rollback path, and PASS/FAIL recommendation.
16. Production is always an independently approved human-triggered operation; staging merge cannot publish to production.
17. Use small commits/PRs, a committed lockfile, deterministic dependency installation and reproducible CI.
18. Stop at milestone review. Do not start the next milestone just because local tests pass.
